{"chapter": "sites", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "04D5", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$f$", "MATH_0002": "$f_*$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:7fca7ceb97709769d8c36aed9d8e37980cd7d7fb123c90e585cf71f9af482598", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:85d84003d95e1d4fef2a32fea14722b296087d4db83234a24f6f0531ecc907c9", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nLet <MATH_0001> be a morphism of topoi. The functor <MATH_0002> in general is only\nleft exact. There are many additional conditions one can impose on this\nfunctor to single out particular classes of morphisms of topoi.\nWe collect them here and note some of the logical dependencies.\nSome parts of the following lemma are purely category theoretical (i.e.,\nthey do not depend on having a morphism of topoi, just having a pair\nof adjoint functors is enough).\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:f707ec41ab279fc7328d87cf36a885b7bdff00fc2a2024be3c58cc413a34797e", "unit_id": "tag:04D5:prose-0001"}
